 We use the concept of modular arithmetic and the given divisor $49$. The divisor $49$ is $7^2$. Hence, we have to consider modulus $7^2=49$.

It is apparent that $8^n$ and $6^n$ are both integers and that $49$ is a prime number. So, we will try to use Fermat's Little Theorem, which states that for a prime number $p$,
\[a^{p-1}\equiv 1\pmod{p}\]
if $a$ and $p$ are coprime, i.e., the only positive integer that divides both of them is $1$.

Firstly, we express $8$ and $6$ in terms of $7$ since we work in modulus $7^2$. We have $8=7+1$ and $6=7-1$. Therefore, by Fermat's Little Theorem,
\begin{align*}
(7+1)^{49}\equiv 1\pmod{49},\\
(7-1)^{49}\equiv 1\pmod{49}.
\end{align*}

So, we convert the base of $8^n$ and $6^n$,

\begin{align*}
8^n &\equiv (7+1)^n\equiv (49k+1)\pmod{49}, \\
6^n &\equiv (7-1)^n\equiv (49k-1)\pmod{49}.
\end{align*}

Hence we can see that
\begin{align*}
a_n = 8^n - 6^n &\equiv (49k+1) - (49k-1)\pmod{49} \\
&\equiv 2\pmod{49}.
\end{align*}

Therefore, we know that $a_{83} \equiv 2 \pmod{49}$. The remainder when $a_{83}$ is divided by $49$ is $\boxed{2}$.

