 We can write the given inequality as $x^3-2x-4>0$. We see that $x=2$ is a root of the cubic $x^3-2x-4$, since $2^3-2\cdot2-4=0$. We can factor this cubic using synthetic division:

$$\begin{array}{c|cc c}
2 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\
  &   & 2 & 4  \\ \hline
  & 1 & 2 & 2  \\
\end{array}$$

So we have $x^3-2x-4=(x-2)(x^2+2x+2)$. Now, $x^2+2x+2$ doesn't factor nicely, but we can see that the quadratic has no real roots since its discriminant is $2^2-4\cdot1\cdot2=-4$.

Therefore, $x^3-2x-4$ has one real root, $x=2$, and so the sign of $x^3-2x-4$ is determined by the factor $x-2$.

So, $x^3-2x>4$ when $x>2$ or in interval notation, $(2,\infty)$.

Alternatively, we could have graphed $y=x^3-2x-4$ to find where the function is positive. The graph of $y=x^3-2x-4$ is shown below.

[asy]
import graph;

real f(real x)
{

return x^3-2x-4;
}

xaxis(Arrows(4));
yaxis(ymax=20,Arrows(4));
draw(graph(f,-3,3),Arrows(4));
dot((2,f(2)), size=8);
label("$(2,-4)$", (2,f(2)), N);
[/asy]

The graph intersects the $x$-axis at $x=2$, so $x^3-2x-4$ changes sign at $x=2$. Therefore, $x^3-2x-4>0$ for $x>2$ or in interval notation, $(2,\infty)$.

So the solution to the inequality $x^3-2x>4$ is $\boxed{(2,\infty)}$.

